WEBVTT

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:07.940
Hi, this is Dr. Jelena Trajkovic, CECS Department, Cal State Long Beach.

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.940
Let us introduce FSMs and their implementation in Verilog.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:19.940
In this video, we are going to look through an example of Moore FSM.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:26.940
And as shown on the diagram here, Moore FSM consists of three separate blocks.

00:00:26.940 --> 00:00:35.980
The first block here is basically a synchronous sequential block that is state register.

00:00:35.980 --> 00:00:41.260
So it's a structure that is going to memorize the state in which FSM is.

00:00:41.260 --> 00:00:47.040
We have two combinational blocks, one of them is next state logic, and the other one is

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:49.380
output logic.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:53.980
We are going to assume that transitions to the next state are going to happening on

00:00:53.980 --> 00:01:00.380
positive edge of the clock. Straight from this diagram, from this organization,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:09.740
three-segment coding style is derived and that is the coding style that we are going to use

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:17.980
in this course. As you can see here you have an always block that is going to represent a basic

00:01:17.980 --> 00:01:25.980
register then you will have always block that is going to represent combinational circuit

00:01:26.780 --> 00:01:36.700
for a state transition basically next state logic and the last one is output generation logic here

00:01:36.700 --> 00:01:44.780
it is written as also another combinational always block but it can be much simpler and

00:01:44.780 --> 00:01:48.940
and simplify itself to a just assignment statement.

00:01:48.940 --> 00:01:53.940
Before all of this, we have a part of code

00:01:55.340 --> 00:02:02.260
in which we show the values basically explicitly

00:02:05.800 --> 00:02:10.800
and declare explicitly assigned values to the states.

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:22.880
states. This portion of code, in general, is optional, as designers can opt out to let

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:31.020
the tool optimize the state assignment. However, in this lab, we are going to look for explicit

00:02:31.020 --> 00:02:40.780
state assignment, and you will have a different encoding than shown here, but the procedure

00:02:40.780 --> 00:02:47.260
is going to be the same, so basically we will have local param which stands for

00:02:47.260 --> 00:02:55.740
local parameter, and we are going to assign explicit values to S0, S1, S2 and S3.

00:02:55.740 --> 00:03:02.820
In this case it's going to be simple binary encoding. So let us look at the

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:10.260
example that we are going to follow through this couple of videos. So the

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:17.300
state transition diagram is shown on the bottom left side. Once again,

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:23.220
we are going to assume that the transitions are happening on every positive edge of the clock cycle

00:03:24.020 --> 00:03:29.380
and transitions are going to start from the starting state, as I rotated here,

00:03:29.380 --> 00:03:33.460
it's going to be S0. Depending on the value of enable,

00:03:33.460 --> 00:03:38.460
the transition is going to be different one.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.600
If enable is one, we will follow this arc

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.460
and we are going to transition from S zero to S one.

00:03:46.460 --> 00:03:50.600
And is if transition, if enable is zero,

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:54.240
we will stay in the same state.

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:00.240
Outputs are shown within the same circle

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:04.560
that annotate the state, as this is more of a same example,

00:04:04.560 --> 00:04:08.640
and we will have a four-bit output sequence.

00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:11.200
It is very similar to one of the examples

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:13.520
that we covered during the lectures.

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:16.880
And in this four-bit sequence,

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:21.640
the number of ones is going to be reducing

00:04:21.640 --> 00:04:25.860
as we move through every state.

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:28.680
So basically from four ones, three ones,

00:04:28.680 --> 00:04:31.780
to two ones and one in a string,

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:36.030
and then back to four of them.

00:04:36.030 --> 00:04:40.990
As I mentioned, the state encoding

00:04:40.990 --> 00:04:45.990
can be left out to tool to be assigned,

00:04:47.250 --> 00:04:50.470
or it can be explicit,

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:53.390
and we are going to use explicit state encoding,

00:04:53.390 --> 00:04:57.370
and we are going to use three-segment code as shown here.

00:04:57.370 --> 00:05:05.370
In the next video, we are going to go through each separate segment in this code

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:12.370
and give more details and tips and tricks on how to successfully write code for this.

00:05:12.370 --> 00:05:17.370
Hope this was helpful. We will continue in the next video.

